.TH SORT 1 "2026" "spicule" "spicule User Commands"
.SH NAME
sort \- sort, merge, or check lines of text
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sort
.RB [ \-bdfinru ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR keydef ]\ .\|.\|.
.RB [ \-t
.IR char ]
.RB [ \-o
.IR output ]
.RI [ file ]\ .\|.\|.
.br
.B sort
.RB [ \-bdfinru ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR keydef ]\ .\|.\|.
.RB [ \-t
.IR char ]
.RB [ \-c | \-C ]
.RI [ file ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sort
reads all of the given
.I file
operands (or standard input, if none are given) fully into memory,
sorts the resulting lines, and writes them to standard output (or to
the file named by
.BR \-o ).
The sort is stable: lines that compare equal keep their original
relative order.
.PP
If no
.B \-k
option is given, the sort key for each line is the entire line.
.PP
Unless
.B \-t
is given, each line is split into fields on runs of one or more
<blank> characters that follow a non-<blank> character; the leading
run of <blank> characters on a line, if any, is included in the first
field rather than stripped. This is not the same field-splitting rule
.BR join (1)
applies by default.
.PP
When two lines compare equal under the active key(s), the tie is
broken by a whole-line, unfiltered byte comparison of the two lines
(honoring
.B \-r
if given, but none of
.BR \-d ,
.BR \-f ,
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-n ,
or any per-key modifier) \(em unless
.B \-u
is given, in which case such a pair is exactly the kind of duplicate
.B \-u
exists to collapse, and no tiebreak comparison is performed for it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Ignore leading <blank> characters when determining the start (and
end) of a comparison.
.TP
.B \-d
Dictionary order: only <blank> and alphanumeric characters are
significant in comparisons.
.TP
.B \-f
Fold lowercase characters to uppercase for comparison purposes.
.TP
.B \-i
Ignore non-printable characters in comparisons.
.TP
.B \-n
Compare numerically: leading <blank> characters and an optional
leading
.B \-
sign are skipped, then as many leading decimal digits as follow are
read as the value to compare; anything after that in the key is not
significant.
.TP
.B \-r
Reverse the sense of comparisons.
.TP
.B \-u
Suppress all but the first of a run of lines with equal keys (unique
by key, not necessarily unique by full line content).
.TP
.BI \-k " keydef"
Restrict comparison to the field range named by
.IR keydef ,
instead of the whole line. May be given more than once; when more
than one
.B \-k
is given, keys are compared in the order given, and the first one
that does not compare equal decides the result.
.IP
.I keydef
has the grammar \fIfield_start\fR[\fItype\fR][,\fIfield_end\fR[\fItype\fR]],
where each of
.I field_start
and
.I field_end
is \fIF\fR[.\fIC\fR] (\fIF\fR a 1-based field number,
.I C
an optional 1-based character position within that field), and
.I type
is zero or more of the letters
.BR b ,
.BR d ,
.BR f ,
.BR i ,
.BR n ,
.B r
attached directly with no separator. Omitting
.I C
on
.I field_start
means the first character of the field; omitting it (or giving 0) on
.I field_end
means the last character of the field. If
.I field_end
is omitted entirely, the key runs from
.I field_start
to the end of the line, not just to the end of that field.
.IP
If any
.I type
letter is attached anywhere in a
.IR keydef ,
that key is compared using only its own attached letters, and none of
the global
.BR \-b / \-d / \-f / \-i / \-n / \-r
options apply to it. A
.B b
letter attached to either
.I field_start
or
.I field_end
is applied to both ends of that key.
.IP
At most 64
.B \-k
options may be given.
.TP
.BI \-t " char"
Use
.I char
as the field separator, instead of the default <blank>-run rule
described above: every occurrence of
.I char
on a line is significant, so adjacent occurrences produce an empty
field between them.
.TP
.BI \-o " output"
Write the sorted result to
.I output
instead of standard output.
.I output
may safely be the same pathname as one of the input
.I file
operands, since every input line is read into memory before
.I output
is opened.
.TP
.B \-c
Check that the single input
.I file
(standard input, if none is given) is already sorted according to the
active options, instead of sorting and writing it. At most one
.I file
operand may be given with
.BR \-c .
If the input is out of order, a diagnostic naming the first
out-of-order line is written to standard error. If
.B \-u
is also given, two adjacent lines with equal keys are likewise
treated as a check failure.
.TP
.B \-C
Same as
.BR \-c ,
but suppresses the diagnostic message; only the exit status reports
the result.
.SH OPERANDS
.TP
.I file
A pathname of an input file, or
.B \-
for standard input. Up to 256 file operands may be given.
.SH APPLICATION USAGE
.B \-m
(merge already-sorted inputs, without re-sorting) is a real
.B sort
option that is not implemented here; it is refused with a diagnostic
rather than silently performing a full sort instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The input was written successfully, or, under
.B \-c
or
.BR \-C ,
the input was already correctly ordered.
.TP
.B 1
Under
.B \-c
or
.BR \-C ,
the input was not correctly ordered, or (with
.B \-u
also given) two lines with equal keys were found.
.TP
.B >1
An error occurred: an invalid option or option argument, too many
.B \-k
options or file operands, more than one file operand with
.B \-c
or
.BR \-C ,
an unreadable input file, an unwritable output file, or an
out-of-memory condition.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uniq (1),
.BR comm (1),
.BR join (1),
.BR tsort (1)
